<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Democratic-Republican Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1792">1792</a>–<a href="/years/1825">1825</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Jeffersonian Republicans</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Democratic-Republican Party was an American political party founded in the early 1790s by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> in opposition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-hamilton">Alexander Hamilton</a>'s Federalists. Favouring states' rights, agrarian interests and a strict reading of the Constitution, it held the presidency from 1801 to 1825 and then split into factions, from one of which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-united-states">Democratic Party</a> emerged.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/democratic-republican-party.jpg" alt="Democratic-Republican Party" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Angelus · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tricolour_Cockade.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party, known at the time as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republican-party-united-states">Republican Party</a>, originated in Congress to oppose the nationalist and economically interventionist policies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-hamilton">Alexander Hamilton</a>, Secretary of the Treasury under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a>. Washington had won the first presidential election of 1788 to 1789 unanimously, at a time when no formal national parties existed. The Republicans and the opposing Federalist Party each became more cohesive during Washington's second term, partly through the debate over the Jay Treaty. Members generally called themselves Republicans and feared that the Federalists, whom they denounced as supporters of monarchy and aristocracy, threatened the principles of republicanism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Jefferson lost the 1796 presidential election to the Federalist John Adams. The Alien and Sedition Acts, passed under Adams, restricted speech critical of the government, and numerous journalists aligned with the Republicans were prosecuted under the Sedition Act, sparking a backlash. Jefferson and Madison drafted the Kentucky and Virginia Resolutions in response. After the elections of 1800 the transfer of power was peaceful. Jefferson's administration eliminated the whiskey excise, shrank the army and navy, repealed the Alien and Sedition Acts, reduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-debt">national debt</a> and completed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a>. Madison, who followed him in 1809, led the country through the War of 1812.</p><p>After the war Madison and his allies established the Second Bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protective-tariff">protective tariffs</a>, a move away from the party's earlier emphasis on states' rights and a strict reading of the Constitution. The Federalists collapsed after 1815, beginning the Era of Good Feelings under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-monroe">James Monroe</a>, who tried to end party conflict. The party was strongest in the South and on the western frontier and weakest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>. It built networks of newspapers in major cities, and its techniques later became standard American practice.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Without an effective opposition the party divided, factions having already formed among moderates, radicals and the Old Republicans, who accused later administrations of betraying republican principles. After the 1824 presidential election it split into rival groups. The coalition built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a> and Martin Van Buren became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-united-states">Democratic Party</a>. The supporters of John Quincy Adams and Henry Clay formed the National Republican Party, which later became part of the Whig Party. Because the party was diverse and changing, both major parties could claim to stand for Jeffersonian principles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> divided it from the start, though in 1807 Congress outlawed the international <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> with Jefferson's support.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Democratic-Republican Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Party politics emerged early, with Federalists against <b>Jeffersonian Republicans</b>, later Democrats against Whigs and, from the 1850s, Democrats against the new Republican Party.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Interest revived in the 1790s, when <b>Jeffersonian Republicans</b> promoted it and Jefferson as its author while Federalists emphasised the whole Congress.</blockquote><a href="/work/united-states-declaration-of-independence">United States Declaration of Independence · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
